  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "What are the alternatives to Chapter 13 bankruptcy?",
      "answer": "Alternatives to Chapter 13 include filing Chapter 7 bankruptcy to liquidate eligible unsecured debts, enrolling in a nonprofit debt management plan, negotiating debt settlements, obtaining a debt consolidation loan, or arranging direct hardship repayment plans with individual lenders."
    },
    {
      "question": "Is a debt management plan better than bankruptcy?",
      "answer": "A debt management plan avoids a formal bankruptcy record on your credit report and allows you to repay your principal balance in full over 36 to 60 months with reduced interest rates. However, a debt management plan does not provide an automatic stay against lawsuits and requires voluntary participation from all your creditors."
    },
    {
      "question": "Will debt settlement hurt my credit less than bankruptcy?",
      "answer": "Debt settlement causes severe credit damage because accounts must typically become delinquent before creditors will negotiate. While delinquent accounts fall off your credit report after seven years under the Fair Credit Reporting Act, Chapter 7 bankruptcy can remain on credit reports for up to 10 years."
    },
    {
      "question": "Do I pay taxes on settled debt?",
      "answer": "Yes, canceled debt of $600 or more is generally treated as taxable income by the IRS and reported on Form 1099-C. You can exclude canceled debt from taxable income only if you qualify for the insolvency exclusion under 26 U.S.C. 108 or file bankruptcy."
    },
    {
      "question": "Can creditors take my Social Security if I don't file bankruptcy?",
      "answer": "Standard consumer debt collectors cannot seize Social Security benefits deposited into your bank account. Under federal regulations, banks must automatically protect two months of direct-deposited federal benefits from account garnishment orders."
    },
    {
      "question": "What happens if I just stop paying my debts?",
      "answer": "If you stop paying, lenders will assess late fees and penalty interest, report delinquencies to credit bureaus, and eventually charge off the debt or sell it to collection agencies. Creditors can also file a lawsuit against you, obtain a court judgment, and seek to garnish non-exempt wages or bank accounts."
    }
  ],
